+# Member-facing conversation access review
+
+Date: 2026-09-07
+
+This is the implementation-plan item 8 review of every member-facing
+conversation detail and action path. It records the boundary in place before
+tasks 019 and 020 make person-level access generic and multi-group aware.
+
+## Required authorization contract
+
+Member-facing conversation reads and actions must be authorized from the
+current person's active group memberships and the root conversation's access
+grants:
+
+- Detail and delivery/receipt reads require active membership in any group with
+  read or write access.
+- In-app follow and unfollow require the same effective read access at action
+  time.
+- In-app reply requires active membership in any group with write access.
+- A root or reply message ID must resolve to the same root conversation before
+  its access grants are evaluated.
+- A matching grant through any one active group is sufficient. Revoking or
+  leaving one group must not remove access supplied independently by another.
+- The selected `group_id` in a URL is return-navigation context, not an access
+  grant. Omitted, stale, or forged rail state must neither grant nor remove
+  effective access.
+- A signed email stop-follow token remains an intentional exception: it may
+  reduce notification state without current membership, but its club,
+  conversation, and member scope must match.
+- Missing resources, foreign-club resources, and conversations for which the
+  current club member has no effective group access must use the ordinary
+  no-disclosure not-found result.
+
+## Route and action inventory
+
+| Surface | Entry point | Current server boundary | Review result |
+| --- | --- | --- | --- |
+| Conversation list link | `GET /groups/:group_id`, then `/messages/:message_id?group_id=...` | `MemberDashboardPresentation` authorizes the selected group; `PageHTML` carries it into the detail URL | The source list is correctly scoped. The query parameter on the destination is navigation context only. |
+| Conversation detail | `GET /messages/:message_id` | `MemberMessageLive.Show` calls `MemberMessageDetail.load/3`, which verifies active selected-club membership and message ownership, then loads only through the deterministic Everyone group | Safe against current private-group disclosure, but it rejects authorized Admin/custom-group members and shared conversations without an Everyone grant. |
+| Live detail refresh | Message, delivery, and follow projection notifications | `MemberMessageLive.Show.refresh_message_detail/1` calls the same loader | It preserves the mount-time Everyone-only rule. It does not react directly to group-membership or access-grant changes. |
+| In-app reply | LiveView `post_reply` event | The sender comes from server assigns. `Messaging.post_message_reply/2` resolves the root and requires active membership in any group with a write grant | The action-time authorization is already group-aware and has the correct any-matching-write-group shape. Detail reachability remains Everyone-only. |
+| In-app follow | LiveView `follow_conversation` event | `Messaging.follow_conversation_as_current_member/2` requires active club membership and a root conversation in that club | It does not require membership in a group that can read the conversation. |
+| In-app unfollow | LiveView `unfollow_conversation` event | `Messaging.unfollow_conversation_as_current_member/2` uses the same club-level predicate as follow | It does not require effective conversation read access. |
+| Per-message delivery and receipts | `GET /messages/:message_id/delivery` for a root or reply | `MemberMessageDeliveryLive.Show` reuses `MemberMessageDetail.load/3`, then queries receipts by message ID | It inherits the safe but Everyone-only gate. The receipt query has no caller authorization of its own, so the shared loader must remain the mandatory web gate. |
+| Receipt-group expansion | LiveView `toggle_receipt_group` event | Changes only socket presentation state after mount | No independent data access occurs; the mounted route's read authorization is the boundary. |
+| Reply-email conversation link | Absolute `/messages/:conversation_id` URL | The destination is the ordinary detail route and carries no selected group | Correct link shape only if detail computes effective person access independently of rail state. |
+| Email stop-follow link | `GET /messages/conversations/stop-following/:token` | `ConversationFollowController` calls `Messaging.stop_following_conversation_from_email_token/2`; the signed token is checked against the existing root's club and conversation | Intentional reduction-only exception. Former members may stop notifications without signing in or retaining group access. |
+| Stop-follow success link | Absolute `/messages/:conversation_id` URL | The destination is the ordinary detail route and carries no selected group | Like reply-email links, it depends on person-level effective access rather than a group query parameter. |
+
+## Existing test evidence
+
+- `member_message_detail_loader_test.exs` proves invalid/unauthorized clubs,
+  missing and foreign-club messages, and the current Everyone-only denial of an
+  Admin conversation.
+- `member_message_live/show_test.exs` covers detail rendering, root/reply
+  delivery links, group-context preservation, and blank-reply behavior.
+- `member_message_live/show_reply_test.exs` covers a server-derived reply
+  sender, in-app follow/unfollow, and denial after club membership ends.
+- `member_message_delivery_live/show_test.exs` covers receipt rendering,
+  foreign-club and non-member denial, root navigation from a reply delivery,
+  and group-context preservation.
+- `messaging/post_message_reply_test.exs` proves reply authorization through an
+  active write-granted group and rejection after group removal, for an
+  unrelated group, and without a write grant.
+- `messaging/message_projection_test.exs` proves the projection can represent a
+  conversation shared with several groups.
+- `conversation_follow_controller_test.exs` covers valid, reused, tampered, and
+  wrong-club stop-follow tokens.
+
+The existing group-context tests use generated IDs to prove URL preservation;
+they do not navigate to an authorized private-group detail or delivery page.
+
+## Changes required by tasks 019 and 020
+
+The smallest coherent implementation seam is a public Messaging authorization
+query that resolves a root or reply message, checks the root's club, obtains
+the groups with the requested access level, and intersects them with
+Membership's public active-group query for the current person and club.
+
+Task 019 can then use that result to:
+
+- replace `MemberMessageDetail`'s Everyone-only load for both detail and
+  delivery;
+- recheck effective read access inside the in-app follow/unfollow boundary;
+- retain the existing write-level reply check; and
+- resolve the signed-in person through a public Membership API rather than
+  matching only the primary email in a club member list.
+
+Task 020 must prove that authorization succeeds through any qualifying active
+group, independent of the selected rail state, including mixed read/write
+grants and loss of one of several qualifying memberships.
+
+Focused tests in task 021 should follow the generated private-group links and
+cover authorized and unauthorized root detail, reply detail, delivery,
+follow/unfollow, direct email links, omitted/forged/stale `group_id`, read-only
+versus write access, and no-disclosure responses.
+
+## Adjacent findings outside this review's implementation slice
+
+- Reply delivery currently selects followers who remain active club members,
+  without rechecking that they still belong to a group that can read the
+  conversation. The iteration explicitly leaves follower-delivery policy out
+  of scope, so this review records the confidentiality risk without changing
+  that policy.
+- The public access-grant commands validate the conversation's club but do not
+  independently verify that the supplied group belongs to that club. There is
+  no member-facing grant-management path in this iteration, but that invariant
+  should be repaired before such a path is introduced.
+- The stop-follow action is a state-changing `GET`; signed scope prevents
+  authority expansion, but automated mail scanners can still exercise a valid
+  link. This behavior predates group-scoped club home.
+
+## Architecture conformance
+
+- ADR 0002 is preserved: read authorization belongs in query/application
+  services, while replies and follow-state changes still dispatch commands.
+- ADR 0005 is preserved: group access decisions do not move recipient
+  resolution into the aggregate.
+- ADR 0007 is preserved: Messaging may coordinate through Membership's public
+  APIs; member-facing web code must not query Membership projection schemas.
+- ADR 0015 is preserved: member detail and delivery remain LiveView surfaces.
+- ADR 0021 is preserved: LiveView refreshes continue to consume committed
+  read-model notifications and re-enter the same authorization loader.
+- ADR 0023 is preserved: `group_id` remains URL-addressable navigation state,
+  while the server computes authority independently on every request/action.
